.TH MKTEMP 1 2026-10-20 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
mktemp \- create a temporary file or directory
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mktemp
.RB [ \-dqtu ]
.RB [ \-p
.IR dir ]
.RB [ \-\-tmpdir [ =\c
.IR dir ]]
.RI [ template ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mktemp
creates a temporary file, or with
.B \-d
a directory, and prints its name on standard output followed by a
newline.
A new file is empty.
.PP
The name is
.I template
with its trailing
.B X
characters replaced to make a name that is not in use.
The template must end in at least six
.B X
characters, and nothing may follow them.
In a file name, and in any name printed by
.BR \-u ,
only the last six are replaced; in a directory name, all of them are.
The rest of the path is left as it is, and its directory part must
already exist, except with
.BR \-u .
With no
.IR template ,
.B mktemp
acts as if it were given
.BR "\-t tmp.XXXXXX" .
.PP
With
.BR \-t ,
.BR \-p ,
or
.BR \-\-tmpdir ,
the template is joined to a base directory by a slash, which is left out
when the base directory already ends in one.
Slashes at the start of the template are dropped first.
The base directory is the last argument given to
.B \-p
or
.BR \-\-tmpdir ,
else the value of
.BR TMPDIR ,
else
.IR /tmp .
Without these options the template is used as given, so a template with
no directory part makes the name in the current directory.
.PP
The name printed is the path as built above, so a relative template
gives a relative name.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-dq ,
and the argument of
.B \-p
may be attached, as in
.B \-p\c
.IR dir .
Options may follow the template, unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
A long option may be shortened to any unambiguous prefix, such as
.B \-\-dry
for
.BR \-\-dry\-run .
At most one template is allowed.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-directory
Create a directory instead of a file.
It has no effect with
.BR \-u ,
which creates nothing.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Print no message when no name can be made, or the file or directory
cannot be created.
The exit status is still 1.
Command line errors are still reported.
.TP
.B \-t
Put the base directory in front of the template.
.TP
.BI "\-p " dir
Use
.I dir
as the base directory.
Implies
.BR \-t .
An empty
.I dir
makes the template relative to the current directory.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-dry\-run
Create nothing.
Print a name that did not exist when it was chosen.
With
.BR \-d ,
the name is made as for a file; see
.BR NOTES .
.TP
\fB\-\-tmpdir\fR[\fB=\fIdir\fR]
Like
.BR \-t .
With
.BI = dir\fR,
use
.I dir
as the base directory, as
.B \-p
does.
The argument must be attached with
.BR = :
a separate word is the template.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument, and any further arguments
are then ignored.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the arguments are expanded when
.B mktemp
starts, as needed when run from
.BR cmd.exe .
A busybox process started with it unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children, so commands run from the kit's shell receive their
arguments as the shell expanded them.
.TP
.B BB_UMASK
When it has the owner write bit,
.BR 200 ,
the file that
.B mktemp
creates is read-only.
A directory is not affected.
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
When set, the C library ends option parsing at the first argument that
is not an option.
.TP
.B TMPDIR
The default base directory.
When it is unset, the value of
.B TMP
is used, then that of
.BR TEMP ,
and Windows normally sets both.
The first one that is set is used even when it is empty, and an empty
value gives
.IR /tmp .
It is used when
.BR \-t ,
.BR \-\-tmpdir ,
or a missing template calls for a base directory, and
.B \-p
or
.BI \-\-tmpdir= dir
gives none.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet, such as
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
for console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Error.
For an invalid command line, meaning an unknown or ambiguous option, a
missing argument to
.BR \-p ,
or more than one template, a usage message is written on standard error.
When no name can be made, or the file or directory cannot be created, a
message of the form
.RB \(dq "mktemp: : " \c
.IR reason \(dq
is written on standard error, unless
.B \-q
is given.
For example, the reason is
.B No such file or directory
when the directory part of the template does not exist,
.B Invalid argument
when the template does not end in six
.B X
characters, and
.B File exists
when the C library runs out of file names; see
.BR NOTES .
.SH EXAMPLES
Create a temporary file, and keep its name in a variable:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ f=$(mktemp)
$ gcc \-E main.c >"$f"
.EE
.RE
.PP
Make a scratch directory that is removed when the shell exits:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ dir=$(mktemp \-d)
$ trap \(aqrm \-rf "$dir"\(aq EXIT
.EE
.RE
.PP
Make a file in another directory:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ mktemp \-p build cc.XXXXXX
build/cc.a14280
.EE
.RE
.PP
Take another action when the directory cannot be made, here because
.I work
does not exist:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ mktemp \-q \-d work/XXXXXX || echo no work directory
no work directory
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU
.BR mktemp :
.IP \(bu 2
The template must end in
.BR XXXXXX ,
and nothing may follow it: there is no
.BR \-\-suffix .
.IP \(bu 2
With
.B \-\-tmpdir
or
.BR \-p ,
a template that starts with a slash is not an error: the slashes are
dropped.
.IP \(bu 2
With
.BR \-t ,
the argument of
.B \-p
takes precedence over
.BR TMPDIR ,
and the template may contain slashes.
.IP \(bu 2
An empty argument to
.B \-p
or
.B \-\-tmpdir=
means the current directory, not the default base directory.
.PP
The C library, not
.BR mktemp ,
chooses the name.
For a file, and for any name printed by
.BR \-u ,
including with
.BR \-d ,
it replaces the six
.B X
characters with a lowercase letter and five digits derived from the ID
of the calling thread, so the names are predictable, not random.
When that name exists, the letter advances through
.BR z ,
and then the attempt fails with
.BR "File exists" .
For a directory, each trailing
.B X
becomes a random letter or digit.
.PP
A file is created after its name is chosen, with no exclusive-create
check, so another process can create the same name in between, and
.B mktemp
then shares the file instead of failing.
Do not rely on
.B mktemp
in a directory that untrusted users can write.
Neither files nor directories are private: access follows the
permissions of the containing directory.
.PP
A slash is the only separator that
.B mktemp
recognizes when it joins the base directory and the template.
A backslash at the end of the base directory gets a slash after it, and
a backslash at the start of the template is kept.
Windows accepts both, so the name can mix separators, as with the usual
.B TMP
setting when
.B mktemp
runs from
.BR cmd.exe :
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
C:\eUsers\eme\eAppData\eLocal\eTemp/tmp.a01276
.EE
.RE
.PP
The kit's shell changes backslashes to slashes in the environment it
imports, so under it
.B TMP
and
.B TEMP
give names with slashes only; see
.BR sh (1).
Elsewhere, set
.B TMPDIR
to a path with forward slashes to avoid mixed separators.
A template that starts with a drive letter, such as
.BR C:/x/XXXXXX ,
cannot follow a base directory.
A base directory that is a bare drive, such as
.IR c: ,
names the current directory of that drive and gets no slash, as in
.IR c:tmp.a01276 .
A template that starts with a slash still gets one after a bare drive,
so the name is in the root of the drive, as in
.IR c:/tmp.a01276 .
.PP
The default base directory
.I /tmp
is not translated.
It names the directory
.I \etmp
on the current drive, and
.B mktemp
does not create it.
.PP
The name is followed by LF, not CRLF.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR mkdir (1),
.BR rm (1),
.BR sh (1)
